   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to mandating training of\ndirect care workers in adverse childhood experiences (ACEs)",
    "basePrintNo": "S2659",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2659-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §20-d, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2659\n\nSPONSOR: SAVINO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the social services law, in relation\nto mandating training of direct care workers in adverse childhood expe-\nriences (ACEs)\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo ensure that all direct-care domestic violence staff undertake\nconsistent state-approved training in Adverse Childhood Experiences\n(ACEs) so that they are better prepared to provide services to clients.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of this bill adds a new section 20-d to the social services\nlaw to require the Office of Temporary and Disability Services (OTDA)\nand the Office of Children and Family Services (OCFS) to contract with\nan entity, or entities in partnership, to develop and provide training\nfor direct-care employees of domestic violence shelters, safe dwellings,\nand domestic violence programs regarded ACEs and to contract with a\ncertifying organization to provide a certification to each direct-care\nemployee who satisfies the requirement of such training program; to\nrequire all direct-care employees of domestic violence shelters, safe\ndwellings, and domestic violence programs to participate in such train-\ning program; and to provide that such training shall be provided at no\ncost to employees.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAdverse Childhood Experiences (ACEs) harm children's developing brains\nso profoundly that the effects show up decades later; they are the cause\nof chronic disease, most mental illness, and are at the root of most\nviolence.\n\n(i)Nearly 20 years ago, the CDC-Kaiser Permanente ACEs study showed that\nmost people have at least one ACE and that people with four or more are\nlikely to experience long-term and chronic health conditions. ACEs\ninclude: physical, emotional, and sexual abuse; physical and emotional\nneglect; parental mental illness, substance abuse, or incarceration;\nparental separation or divorce; and domestic violence.\n\n(ii)(ii) ACEs literally change the brain architecture and impair the\nprefrontal cortex, the home of Executive Function (EF)-our ability to\nmake decisions, self-regulate, set and achieve goals, and plan for the\nfuture. With the brain always on high alert, there is no room for these\nbasic skills to develop. Therefore, families experiencing ACEs and\ndiminished EF struggle to succeed in society.\n\n\nNew York State currently offers no consistent ACES training; a mix of\nnon-profits offer various trainings to frontline staff such as social\nworkers. This is not a consistent approach and no curriculum is\nmandated. The development, adoption, and implementation of an approved\ntraining will result in a comprehensive approach, and with certified\ntrained staff across the State.\n\nDomestic violence shelters, safe dwellings, and domestic violence\nprograms are ideal places to pilot this training. Directors and staff\nsee the day-to-day impacts of ACEs on two generations-the mother and\nchild(ren). Yet they may not know exactly what they are seeing, or how\nto address it appropriately. A consistent training would lay the founda-\ntion for staff to understand the prevalence of ACEs, learn about efforts\nto reduce and prevent them, and start to understand the long-term conse-\nquences of ACEs and the associations between ACES and EF, health risk\nbehaviors, and chronic conditions.\n\nThe Office of Temporary and Disability Assistance (OTDA) and the Office\nof Children and Family Services (OCFS) would approve a nonprofit (or\ncollaborative group of nonprofits) to provide curriculum and training.\nThis entity would have experience in domestic violence, child abuse, and\nACEs policy/practice. The New York State Coalition Against Domestic\nViolence, the organization designated by the federal Department of\nHealth and Human Services to coordinate statewide improvements within\nlocal communities, social service systems, and programming regarding the\nprevention and intervention of domestic violence in New York, will\ncertify completion of this program. There would be no cost to partic-\nipants.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2018 Session New bill - S.7756-A referred to Social Services\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTo be determined\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n180 days after it shall have become law\n",
